The file is right there in your editor, yet the terminal reports
that it does not exist. You open another window and now the
command itself is missing. These are different failures. Changing
permissions would address neither if the original shell was in the
wrong directory and the new shell had a different search path.
The quickest useful action is not the biggest repair: find an
observation that separates two plausible causes.

\begin{objectives}
\item Distinguish input, command lookup, working directory, and
remote-session problems.
\item Recover usable input without unnecessarily terminating a process.
\item Reproduce and fix a missing command and a missing file using
only disposable fixtures.
\end{objectives}

\section{Read the symptom before repeating the command}\label{sec:45-triage}

Start by naming what you saw: ``I got a continuation prompt
after pressing Enter,'' ``nothing new was displayed,'' or ``the
shell printed command not found.'' The words are not a diagnosis.
``Nothing new was displayed'' might mean a command finished
silently, a command is waiting for input, a terminal stopped
displaying new output after a flow-control key, or a remote
session is no longer responsive. Look for the prompt you
normally see. If you do not have access to the display or
scrollback, use a known review route from Chapter~\ref{ch:44}
rather than entering commands at a guessed input position.

\begin{quicktable}{p{.32\linewidth}Y}
\toprule
Observation & Question to answer next \\
\midrule
Continuation prompt & Is a quote, parenthesis, or continuation
backslash waiting for the rest of the command? \\
No visible prompt & Is an application still in the foreground,
or was output paused? \\
Command not found & Which shell is looking up which exact name
in which environment? \\
File not found & Which working directory and which filename
did the command use? \\
Permission denied & Which operation and exact path failed?
Which machine and account own them? \\
\bottomrule
\end{quicktable}

Figure~\ref{fig:45-triage} starts with the smallest question: who
would receive the next key? When an editor holds unsaved work,
quit or save using the editor's own commands. When a pager has
the keyboard, use its documented quit key. If you knowingly
started a harmless foreground command that is waiting, an interrupt
may be reasonable. \KeyChord{Ctrl}{C} typically sends an interrupt
to the foreground process group when the terminal handles that
key in its usual mode. It is not a universal undo. A partly copied
file can remain partly copied. If you do not know what is running,
do not use repeated interrupts or close the entire window as a
first guess. Moving the investigation to a spare local window
can preserve an important process in the first.

\input{figures/chapter-45/triage.tikz}

\section{Recover input and display separately}

Here is a safe input practice in a spare interactive POSIX-style
shell. This deliberately unfinished input ends in a single
unclosed quote; it starts no external program. Type the line
and press \EnterKey{}. You may see a secondary prompt such as
\texttt{>}, hear a cue, or see a new input line. Use
\KeyChord{Ctrl}{C} to cancel the incomplete input, then enter
the second block only once at the main prompt. If the spare shell
does not respond to the interrupt, there are no practice files
yet; leave the window open and try a known local terminal
configuration instead.

\begin{terminal}
printf '%s\n' 'unfinished
\end{terminal}

\begin{terminal}
printf '%s\n' 'ready for input'
\end{terminal}

Seeing ``ready for input'' shows this shell accepted a complete
command. It does not say anything about another window.
When output is paused because software flow control is enabled
and the stop character was sent, the configured start character,
commonly \KeyChord{Ctrl}{Q}, can resume display. Do not use that
key blindly in an editor, an application that binds it to Quit,
or a screen reader that handles it first. Flow control may be
disabled, may use other keys, or may not explain the symptom.
Read the existing terminal settings at a confirmed shell prompt:

\begin{terminal}
stty -a
\end{terminal}

\cmd|stty| gets or changes terminal line settings; \cmd|-a| prints
them in readable form. Look for the start and stop characters,
normally shown with caret notation such as \texttt{\^{}Q}, and
whether \texttt{ixon} is enabled (a leading minus indicates it
is disabled). The output is implementation-dependent. Standard
input needs to be connected to the intended terminal for this
check; do not run \cmd|stty -a < ./somefile| and expect it to
inspect the window. For a verified local shell with broken
echo or line input after a tool exits, \cmd|stty sane| is a
recovery option, not a read-only probe. It sets many defaults
and can change settings you deliberately chose. If the wrong
application still owns input, neither it nor \cmd|reset| will
run as a shell command. Use the app's documented recovery method
or a separate working window. Do not practice intentionally
breaking a terminal needed for work.
\IndexCommand{stty}\IndexTerm{terminal!flow control}

\section{Two failures that can look alike}\label{sec:45-path}

Use POSIX shell syntax below; start a child \cmd|sh| if needed.
The practice creates a helper and a report in a fresh directory.
Stop if setup fails, before performing any writes. The helper
only prints a sentence. It stands in for a trusted program you
know should be runnable. Its name, \texttt{utp45-report}, is
chosen to reduce the chance of colliding with anything installed.

\begin{terminal}
lab=$(mktemp -d "${TMPDIR:-/tmp}/utp45.XXXXXX") && cd "$lab"
pwd
mkdir ./work ./other
printf '%s\n' 'Inventory: four notebooks' > ./work/report.txt
printf '%s\n' '#!/bin/sh' "printf '%s\n' 'helper ran'" \
    > ./utp45-report
cat ./utp45-report
chmod u+x ./utp45-report
\end{terminal}

The first line of the helper selects \texttt{/bin/sh} on the
systems discussed here; the second is the only command it runs.
\cmd|chmod u+x| adds owner execute permission to this exact
practice file (Chapter~\ref{ch:33}). Neither superuser privileges
nor write permission for other users is needed. If the
temporary filesystem disallows direct execution, do not change
its mount settings: you can read the code and use
\cmd|sh ./utp45-report| instead. The same helper is supplied as
\FilePath{listings/chapter-45/utp45-report}.

First read the report from a different working directory.
Parentheses run the enclosed list in a subshell. The working
directory change stays inside it. Use \cmd|&&| so that if
entering the directory fails, the reading commands do not run.
The semicolon ends the first inner command. \cmd|cat| reads the
filename relative to that subshell.

\begin{terminal}
(cd ./other && { pwd; cat ./report.txt; })
printf 'attempt status: %s\n' "$?"
pwd
cat ./work/report.txt
\end{terminal}

The subshell prints a path ending in \texttt{/other}; then
\cmd|cat| diagnoses the missing file. The status is nonzero.
The second \cmd|pwd| shows the original directory. The working
read prints ``Inventory: four notebooks.'' A missing report
diagnostic is evidence that \cmd|cat| was found and ran. It is
not evidence that \cmd|cat| needs reinstalling. Inspect the
path in the diagnostic and compare it to the path you expect
before thinking about permissions or storage devices.

\input{figures/chapter-45/paths.tikz}

Now try lookup of the helper. The \cmd|PATH| value here is
the full path of the empty practice directory \texttt{other}.
Setting it only for a subshell guarantees that the helper in the
parent directory is outside the search. It also hides ordinary
external utilities in that subshell; \cmd|command| and \cmd|printf|
are built in on the shells used here. The alias and function
exceptions are one reason to use a distinctive name and a spare
shell. You should not temporarily point your real shell's path at
an untrusted directory to diagnose a missing name.

\begin{terminal}
(PATH="$lab/other"; command -v utp45-report
 printf 'lookup status: %s\n' "$?")
./utp45-report
\end{terminal}

Expect no resolved path from the lookup and a nonzero lookup
status. The direct execution should print ``helper ran'' unless
the filesystem disallows execute. An explicit path contains
a slash; it does not need a search through \cmd|PATH|. Both
examples are shown in Figure~\ref{fig:45-paths}. A command may
also resolve to an alias, function, or builtin. Read what
\cmd|command -v| returns; do not assume every resolution prints
an absolute executable path. For a genuinely missing optional
tool, check its documented installation and your platform's
package mechanism (Chapter~\ref{ch:35}); reinstalling is not
the starting move when the name was simply mistyped.

\begin{dialoguebox}{The wrong repair}
\Brian{Both times it said something wasn't found. Why separate them?}
\Stephen{In one case the shell couldn't locate the command.}
\Brian{But the other error also came out of the terminal.}
\Stephen{True. The terminal showed both. The second came from a command that had already started.}
\Brian{Then reinstalling that command would leave it looking in the wrong directory.}
\Stephen{Exactly. Check what issued the diagnostic before choosing a repair.}
\Brian{I can test the directory without changing anything.}
\DialogueConclusion{Find the source of the error first.}
\end{dialoguebox}

\section{Check the machine before changing the machine}

A local shell and a remote shell can display identical prompts.
Check at a confirmed shell prompt, then compare what you see
with the expected context you noted earlier. \cmd|uname -n|
reports the network node name; \cmd|id -un| asks for the
effective user name, not the complete list of identifiers and
groups. These two strings and a working directory are useful
clues but not secure proof of a machine's identity. In particular,
trust the SSH host verification from Chapter~\ref{ch:38}, not a
hostname printed after connecting. Do not post real usernames,
machine names, or complete home paths when sharing a diagnostic.

\begin{terminal}
uname -n
id -un
pwd
command -v cat
\end{terminal}

\input{figures/chapter-45/contexts.tikz}

Figure~\ref{fig:45-contexts} uses different directory and
account context on each side of a connection. A failure before
authentication may involve name resolution, routing, or the
remote service; a failure after authentication may instead be
in a command on the far end. Verify the boundary before trying
the command again. If a host-key warning appears, stop and check
the change using an independent trusted channel; do not silence
the check. If you suspect a stalled remote connection, preserve
what you saw and open a second local window to diagnose, instead
of entering more remote commands without seeing results.
Closing a client is not a promise that the server stopped the
work; Chapter~\ref{ch:40} covers detachable sessions.

\begin{quicktable}{lY}
\toprule
Context clue & What it cannot prove \\
\midrule
Prompt label & The prompt may be customized or duplicated on another machine. \\
User name & The same name may exist on more than one machine. \\
Working directory & Identical path text can refer to distinct filesystems. \\
Node name & A name reported by a shell is not an authenticated host key. \\
\bottomrule
\end{quicktable}

\begin{takeaways}
\item Check which layer is receiving input before attempting a repair.
\item Distinguish command lookup from the paths a running command opens.
\item Prefer one low-impact test to a bundle of speculative changes.
\end{takeaways}

\begin{exercise}{Fix a missing file, not the command}\label{ex:45-file}
From inside a subshell that changes into \texttt{other}, read the
report in \texttt{work} by a relative path, without moving or
copying it. Verify that the parent shell remains in the practice
directory. No new file is needed.
\end{exercise}
\begin{solution}
\begin{terminal}
(cd ./other && cat ../work/report.txt)
pwd
\end{terminal}
The relative path walks up to the fixture root and down into
\texttt{work}. The line of inventory appears and the parent
working directory is unchanged.
\end{solution}

\begin{exercise}{A name versus a path}\label{ex:45-name}
In a subshell with the empty search path directory used above,
compare \texttt{command -v} for the helper's bare name and its
explicit relative path. Do not execute it in this exercise.
\end{exercise}
\begin{solution}
\begin{terminal}
(PATH="$lab/other"; command -v utp45-report
 command -v ./utp45-report)
\end{terminal}
Expect a miss, then a path for the executable on the tested
shells. An execute restriction can prevent use of the path;
look at the exact diagnostic if you get a different result.
The parent path setting and files are unmodified.
\end{solution}

\begin{exercise}{A small report for someone else}\label{ex:45-report}
In the spare shell, gather the exact command and observable
result for the failed report read. Capture standard error in
\texttt{problem.txt}. Replace the temporary root path with
the words ``practice directory'' if it appears in the report
you would share. Clean up that file at the end.
\end{exercise}
\begin{solution}
\begin{terminal}
(cd ./other && cat ./report.txt) 2> ./problem.txt
printf 'attempt status: %s\n' "$?"
cat ./problem.txt
\end{terminal}
The shell opens the diagnostic file in the parent practice
directory before entering \texttt{other}. Verify a nonzero
status and the diagnostic about \texttt{./report.txt}; wording
varies by tool and locale. Report that the same read succeeded
from the parent using \texttt{./work/report.txt}. Leave out
irrelevant diagnostics and identifiers.
\end{solution}

Cleanup: use \cmd|pwd| to confirm the original practice
directory. Omit \texttt{problem.txt} if you skipped that exercise.
If a directory is not empty, inspect it rather than increasing
the power of the removal command.

\begin{terminal}
rm ./work/report.txt ./utp45-report ./problem.txt
rmdir ./work ./other
cd / && rmdir "$lab"
\end{terminal}

